treatment:
What are the treatments for Hyperlipoproteinemia?
    Long-term comprehensive treatment should be adhered to, emphasizing on the control of diet and physical exercise as the basis, combined with lipid-lowering drug therapy. For secondary cases (such as diabetes, hypothyroidism), the primary disease should be actively treated.
    1. Diet and exercise therapy
    The goal is to reduce plasma cholesterol and maintain balanced nutrition. Exercise and weight reduction not only help in lowering cholesterol but also reduce triglycerides and increase HDL cholesterol. Diet therapy and lifestyle improvements are foundational measures in the treatment of dyslipidemia. Whether or not lipid-lowering drug treatment is undertaken, it is imperative to control diet and improve lifestyle.
    2. Pharmacotherapy
    According to the type of dyslipidemia and the therapeutic goals, suitable lipid-lowering drugs are selected. Regular monitoring of the efficacy of lipid-lowering treatment and drug side effects is necessary.
    Medications for treating hyperlipidemia primarily include:
    (1) HMG-CoA Reductase Inhibitors (Statins): Currently, the most widely used class of drugs in clinical applications. Available statins include Simvastatin, Atorvastatin, Rosuvastatin, typically recommended for evening intake.
    (2) Fibric Acid Derivatives (Fibrates);
    (3) Niacin;
    (4) Bile Acid Sequestrants;
    (5) Cholesterol Absorption Inhibitors;
    (6) Cholesterol Synthesis Inhibitors;
    (7) n-3 Fatty Acids, Omega-3 Long-Chain Polyunsaturated Fatty Acids;
    (8) Probucol.

Treatment options for Multiple Organ Dysfunction Syndrome include: 
(1) Prompt and effective resuscitation, debridement to prevent infection, prevention of ischemia-reperfusion injury, and various supportive treatments; 
(2) Mitigate the stress response, reducing both the extent and duration of hypermetabolism and glucocorticoid receptor activation; 
(3) Prioritize respiratory and circulatory support, correcting hypovolemia and hypoxia at an early stage; 
(4) Preventing infection is a crucial measure in preventing MODS; 
(5) Improve the patient's overall nutritional status as much as possible; 
(6) Timely treatment of any initial organ failures. 
Emergency medicine experts caution that MODS is a complication of severe infections, severe trauma, and other critical conditions, characterized by the failure of vital organs such as the heart, liver, and kidneys, as well as potential impairment of gastrointestinal and central nervous system functions, and the occurrence of Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC), among others. The progression from Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome (SIRS) to MODS can include concurrent or sequential occurrences of Acute Lung Injury (ALI), acute renal failure, DIC, acute gastrointestinal bleeding, with an overarching inflammatory response throughout the process.
